# SRG-APP-000001-WSR-000002  -  CAT II
# The web server must perform server-side session management.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000001-WSR-000002
group:           V-206351
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-000054
control_rev5:    AC-10

contribution:    Splunk Web keeps session state on the server rather than in the client,
                 and the application configures that directly: tools.sessions.on turns
                 server-side session support on, tools.sessions.storage_type and
                 storage_path place the session store on the server, tools.sessions.timeout
                 lets the server expire an idle session, and tools.sessions.httponly and
                 tools.sessions.secure keep the session identifier out of client-side
                 script and off cleartext transport.

governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.storage_type equals file
check:           web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.storage_path non_empty
check:           web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.timeout min 2
check:           web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.httponly in 1|true
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.on https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.secure https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.timeout https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/manage-users/configure-user-session-timeouts
retired_check:   web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.on equals true
                 reason: The spec states the setting as a literal rather than a type:
                         "tools.sessions.on = true. Whether or not user session support is
                         enabled. Always set this to true." No default is stated.
retired_check:   web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.secure in 1|true
                 reason: "Whether or not the browser must transmit session cookies over an
                         HTTPS connection when Splunk Web is configured to serve requests
                         using HTTPS (the 'enableSplunkWebSSL' setting is 'true'.) If set to
                         'true' and 'enableSplunkWebSSL' is also 'true', then the browser
                         must transmit the session cookie over HTTPS connections. This
                         improves session security. Default: true"
# --- end vendor verification

external:        The inactivity period itself is organization-defined; the criterion tests
                 only that the application expresses a server-side timeout.
external:        Where a reverse proxy terminates TLS in front of Splunk Web, the secure
                 bit on the cookie returned to the client depends on
                 tools.sessions.forceSecure together with the proxy's own configuration, so
                 the proxy carries part of this objective.
external:        Filesystem permissions protecting the session store directory are carried
                 by the operating system.

citation:        splunk-spec-files/web.conf.spec:16 ([settings]), 920-922
                 (tools.sessions.on = true, "Always set this to true"), 924-933
                 (tools.sessions.timeout, default 60, "Use a value of 2 or higher"),
                 943-949 (tools.sessions.httponly, default true), 951-960
                 (tools.sessions.secure, default true), 962-970
                 (tools.sessions.forceSecure, default false), 977-982
                 (tools.sessions.storage_type = [file], storage_path, default
                 storage_type=file, storage_path=var/run/splunk); SRG-APP-000001-WSR-000002
                 check and fix text.
finding:         Splunk Web is not configured for server-side session management, because
                 one or more of web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.on,
                 tools.sessions.storage_type, tools.sessions.storage_path,
                 tools.sessions.timeout, tools.sessions.httponly, or tools.sessions.secure
                 is unset or set to a weaker value; this is assessed at the application
                 layer alone, and cookie handling by any TLS-terminating proxy in front of
                 Splunk Web is recorded as an external dependency rather than tested here.
